Master Prompt: Liberia Private-School Operations Platform
Version: 1.0  
Purpose: Product discovery, architecture, implementation, testing, and deployment of a configurable school operations platform for private and faith-based schools in Liberia.
---
Copy-and-use prompt
You are a senior product engineer, software architect, UX designer, security engineer, QA lead, and implementation partner. Help design and build a production-quality Private-School Operations Platform for schools in Liberia. The platform must be simple enough for busy school administrators and teachers to use, dependable on low-bandwidth connections, configurable across different schools, and designed to exchange education data with Liberia’s Ministry of Education (MoE) EMIS when approved formats and integration mechanisms are available.
Do not treat this as a generic school website, a spreadsheet replacement with no workflow, or a Ministry EMIS replacement. It is an operational system for an individual school or school group, with controlled, transparent reporting to the Ministry.
1. Product goals
Build a secure, mobile-friendly web application that helps a private school manage its complete administrative and academic cycle:
Register and admit prospective learners.
Maintain accurate, longitudinal learner records.
Organize academic years, terms, classes, subjects, timetables, and teacher assignments.
Record attendance and manage absences, lateness, and follow-up.
Enter, moderate, approve, publish, and report grades.
Assess tuition and other charges, record payments, track balances, issue receipts, and reconcile collections.
Communicate appropriately with parents and guardians.
Manage staff profiles, attendance, assignments, and basic HR records.
Produce useful school, academic, financial, and operational reports.
Capture school identity and education data needed for MoE reporting and future EMIS exchange.
Prioritize accuracy, ease of use, reliability, auditability, privacy, and low-cost operation. Avoid adding features that increase complexity without a clear school user, workflow, and measurable benefit.
2. Target users and permission roles
Implement role-based access control. A person may hold more than one role, but every permission must be explicit and enforced by the server, not merely hidden in the user interface.
Platform operator: manages tenant provisioning and platform health; must not routinely inspect school records.
School owner / board administrator: institution-wide oversight, approved reports, and configuration.
Principal / head of school: academic and operational oversight; approves defined actions.
Registrar / admissions officer: applications, enrollment, learner records, transfers, and document tracking.
Bursar / finance officer: fees, invoices, receipts, balances, discounts, bursaries, and reconciliation.
Teacher: assigned classes and subjects; attendance and marks entry only for assigned learners and periods.
Department head / academic coordinator: subject setup, grade review, moderation, and academic reports within scope.
HR / staff administrator: staff records, contracts, assignments, and staff attendance.
Parent / guardian: only their linked learners’ notices, attendance summaries, results when released, fees/balances, and receipts.
Learner: age-appropriate access to their own timetable, work, attendance, and released results if the school enables it.
Auditor / read-only reviewer: permitted records and reports, with logged access and no editing.
Support least privilege, school-level and campus-level scope, temporary access, account deactivation, password reset, and access review. Prevent a staff member at one school from accessing any other school’s data. Do not create shared default passwords.
3. Multi-school architecture and configuration
Design the application as a multi-tenant SaaS product with strict tenant data isolation. One school group may have multiple campuses, but each school controls its own records, users, policies, academic calendar, fee structure, grading scale, branding, and communication preferences.
Create a configuration layer for:
School name, ownership/type, campuses, school year, academic calendar, terms, and holidays.
Education levels and class/grade naming. Do not hard-code one school’s labels or grade structure.
Sections/streams, class sizes, subjects, grading scales, pass rules, promotion rules, and report-card formats.
Fee items, due dates, payment plans, approved discounts, bursaries, penalties, and receipt numbering.
Attendance codes, late-arrival rules, absence escalation, and acceptable approval workflows.
Parent communication channels, language, consent, notification templates, and quiet hours.
Required learner documents and admissions criteria.
MoE school identifiers, reporting periods, data mappings, and export templates.
Support school branding (logo, colors, letterhead/receipt settings), with accessible contrast and a consistent shared design system.
4. Core functional requirements
A. School setup and onboarding
Guided setup for a new school, campus, academic year, grading system, fee schedule, classes, subjects, and user roles.
Capture institution name, school type/ownership, levels offered, address, county, district, contact details, and MoE school EMIS code.
Permit schools to record their Ministry permit status, permit number, issue/expiry dates, and related document references.
Import learners, guardians, staff, classes, and balances from validated CSV/XLSX templates. Provide preview, field mapping, validation errors, duplicate detection, and rollback before committing imports.
Include a sample dataset/demo mode clearly separated from production tenant data.
B. Admissions and enrollment
Prospect/application intake, application reference, learner and guardian contacts, requested level/class, admission status, decision, and notes.
Configurable required documents and checklist, such as prior school records, birth documentation where required, photographs, and transfer records.
Application review, interview/assessment appointment, offer, acceptance, waitlist, rejection, and enrollment conversion.
Prevent accidental duplicate learner profiles using configurable matching signals; do not merge records automatically without review.
Capture transfer-in/transfer-out, withdrawal, graduation/completion, and re-enrollment with dates and reasons.
Keep a clear distinction between prospect, enrolled learner, withdrawn learner, and archived record.
C. Student records
Maintain a longitudinal learner profile with:
Unique internal learner number and optional Ministry/national learner identifier fields.
Legal/preferred names, date of birth, sex/gender fields according to approved policy, photo where appropriate, and contact information.
Guardian relationships, emergency contacts, authorized pickup persons, and communication preferences.
Enrollment history, campus, class/section, status, prior schools, and transfer history.
Medical or accommodation information only when necessary, with restricted access and explicit purpose; never expose sensitive notes in general class lists or parent messages.
Document attachments with category, date, visibility, and retention controls.
Record correction history and who changed each sensitive field.
Allow schools to define data-retention policies. Support export and deletion/anonymization workflows subject to school policy and applicable law. Do not promise legal compliance without confirming the relevant rules with the customer.
D. Academic structure and teaching
Set up academic years, terms, class/sections, subjects, rooms, teacher assignments, and timetable periods.
Support teacher substitution, timetable conflict checking, and printable/downloadable timetables.
Maintain curriculum/topic coverage or lesson-planning links as an optional module; do not force schools to use it.
Provide a class/subject workspace showing only the learners and activities assigned to that teacher.
E. Attendance
Daily attendance by class/section and configurable session (for example, morning/afternoon or period-level).
Fast roster interface suitable for phones and tablets, with present, absent, late, excused, and other school-configured codes.
Prevent duplicate attendance submissions for the same learner/session; permit corrections with reason and audit entry.
Record attendance offline on a device where feasible, then sync safely when connected; clearly show pending sync and conflicts.
Automated absence and lateness alerts to authorized guardians only, respecting school rules and communication consent.
Attendance summaries by learner, class, subject, date range, and school year; flag concerning patterns for staff review without making unsupported diagnoses.
F. Grades, assessments, and reports
Configurable assessment types, weights, grading scales, term structure, subject mark components, pass thresholds, and comments.
Marks entry by assigned teacher with draft, submit, return-for-correction, moderation, approval, and publication states.
Validate ranges and weights; clearly identify missing marks and learners who should not be graded for a defined reason.
Lock approved results; require permission and a reason to reopen or change them.
Generate report cards with attendance, marks, grade interpretation, teacher comments, promotion status, school branding, and approval signatures/fields.
Support learner transcripts and year-to-year history.
Do not use AI to decide admissions, promotion, discipline, or learner ability. Any optional AI-generated summaries must be clearly labeled, reviewed by a human, and based only on authorized data.
G. Fees, payments, and balances
Configure tuition and other charges by school year, level/class, learner, and approved category.
Create statements, invoices, due dates, instalment schedules, approved discounts, bursaries/scholarships, credits, and adjustments.
Record payment channel, date, amount, currency, transaction/reference number, cashier, and receipt number. Support USD and Liberian dollars (LRD) as separate currencies; never silently mix or convert amounts.
Provide cash receipt print/PDF, payment history, learner balance, class collection summary, aging report, and daily cashier close-out.
Support reversals/refunds with approval and reason; never delete financial transactions from the audit history.
Design payment-provider integrations as optional adapters. Do not claim a mobile-money or bank connection until provider access, credentials, and test results exist.
Restrict financial access to authorized users and avoid exposing another family’s balances in parent accounts or bulk lists.
H. Parent and guardian communications
Guardian portal with linked learners, notices, attendance summaries, released report cards, fee statements, and receipts.
Message templates for announcements, absences, fee reminders, school closures, and parent meetings.
Notification preferences and consent records. Provide delivery status where the channel supports it.
Support email, in-app notification, and SMS/WhatsApp through separately configured providers. Never send messages through a provider without school authorization and configured credentials.
Prevent accidental disclosure by validating recipients and the linked learner before sending individualized messages.
Provide an announcement board for school-wide or class-specific communication, with publisher and approval workflow.
I. Staff records and school HR
Staff directory, role, campus, subjects/classes, employment dates/status, contact details, qualifications, emergency contact, and document checklist.
Staff attendance and leave records as configurable basic modules.
Assign teachers to class/subject and retain assignment history.
Optional appraisal, payroll, and recruitment modules must be separate, permission-controlled extensions. Do not calculate statutory payroll deductions or claim legal payroll compliance without locally validated rules.
J. Reports and dashboards
Provide useful role-specific dashboards, with date filters, downloadable reports, and visible data freshness:
Enrollment by class/level, new admissions, transfers, withdrawals, and retention.
Attendance by learner, class, teacher, and date range.
Assessment completion, grade distribution, promotion outcomes, and missing marks.
Fees billed, collected, outstanding, payment aging, discounts/bursaries, and cashier reconciliation.
Staff and teacher assignments, attendance, and class coverage.
School census and administrative statistics that can be mapped to MoE reporting templates.
Reports must respect the requesting user’s permissions and tenant scope. Use clear definitions for totals and percentages; do not silently exclude missing or inactive records.
5. Ministry of Education EMIS compatibility
Build an EMIS compatibility layer, not an assumed direct integration. Public Ministry materials show an MoE EMIS for school statistics and reports, a school permit system that captures the school EMIS code and location/contact information, and current work to strengthen EMIS policy and national data standards. A national learner and teacher identifier system has been discussed; treat those identifiers as optional external IDs until MoE confirms their official form, issuance, and use.
Implement the following:
Canonical internal model: maintain normalized school, campus, learner, guardian, teacher/staff, class, enrollment, attendance, and reporting-period records.
External identifier fields: store the MoE school EMIS code; include optional, nullable external learner and teacher IDs. Do not generate Ministry IDs or reuse the internal learner number as an official Ministry ID.
Versioned mapping registry: allow authorized administrators to map internal fields to a Ministry schema/template. Track schema name, version, effective date, mapping owner, and change history.
Export first: provide validated CSV/XLSX exports and printable summaries using the latest Ministry-approved templates supplied by the school or Ministry. Include an export preview, missing-field report, row-level validation, and a downloadable error file.
Safe import: allow authorized imports of MoE school/learner/teacher reference data only from approved files or interfaces. Preview changes, identify conflicts, and require confirmation before updating records.
Adapter boundary: create a documented interface for a future official API or secure file exchange. Implement a live connector only after the Ministry provides specifications, credentials, permission, and a test environment. Keep integration secrets out of source code and logs.
No scraping or unauthorized access: do not automate login to Ministry portals, scrape records, write to Ministry systems, or represent an export as submitted unless an authorized integration confirms receipt.
Reconciliation: record export/import history, who initiated it, time, template version, row counts, validation results, and any receipt/acknowledgment from an approved external system.
Change readiness: make mappings configurable because EMIS forms, annual census fields, policy, and national IDs may change. Keep historical exports reproducible by preserving the template and mapping version used.
Data minimization: only include fields required for the applicable Ministry report; do not export sensitive medical, family, or financial notes unless explicitly required and authorized.
Before implementing a Ministry connector, inspect current official documentation and ask the school/customer to provide any unpublished reporting template or written authorization. If there is no verified interface, ship export/import workflows and label the integration status clearly as “file-based” or “not connected.”
6. Modern product features
Implement these as purposeful enhancements, prioritized after dependable core workflows:
Progressive web app (PWA) behavior, responsive phone/tablet/desktop layouts, and installable home-screen access.
Low-bandwidth optimization, resilient loading, draft autosave, and safe offline capture for attendance or marks where technically feasible.
Notifications and configurable reminders with delivery logs.
Accessibility: keyboard support, readable font sizes, clear contrast, screen-reader labels, and plain language.
English interface initially; structure translations so Liberian/local language support can be added after user validation.
In-app help, onboarding checklists, sample data, contextual guidance, and administrator training materials.
Search across authorized school records with exact permissions and audit controls.
Document scanning/upload, safe file validation, and configurable retention.
Data export and school-owned backups; provide a clear school offboarding/export path.
Optional learning resources/LMS links, library, school bus/transport, boarding/house management, cafeteria, inventory, and alumni modules only after product discovery confirms demand. Keep them independently enableable.
Optional AI assistance may help draft non-sensitive announcements or summarize school-level trends. Require review before publishing, avoid personal-data use unless explicitly enabled and controlled, and never present generated content as an official decision.
7. Security, privacy, and reliability requirements
Enforce tenant isolation in database queries and background jobs; test cross-tenant access denial.
Use secure authentication, strong password handling, MFA option for privileged accounts, session expiry, rate limiting, and account lockout protections.
Apply server-side role and object-level authorization to every read and write.
Encrypt traffic in transit and sensitive stored data where supported by the selected infrastructure.
Maintain immutable audit events for logins, sensitive record access, learner data changes, grade approvals/changes, finance actions, exports, role changes, and configuration updates.
Avoid storing passwords, payment credentials, or unnecessary sensitive data in logs. Protect uploaded files from unsafe types and unauthorized access.
Provide backups, restore procedures, monitoring, error reporting without exposing learner information, and documented incident response steps.
Design graceful behavior for slow internet, intermittent electricity, browser refreshes, duplicate submissions, and device loss.
Use secure development practices, dependency review, secrets management, and production/staging separation.
Define and document data ownership, hosting location, backups, retention, support access, and offboarding with each customer.
8. Technical delivery approach
First inspect the existing repository, hosting environment, framework, and available integrations. Do not replace working infrastructure or choose a new stack before understanding the current project. If there is no project, propose a maintainable architecture and explain the choice briefly before implementation.
Use a modular monolith or similarly simple architecture unless verified scale or integration needs justify distributed services. Keep clear boundaries between identity/tenancy, academics, admissions, attendance, finance, communications, HR, reports, and EMIS exchange. Document the data model and key decisions.
Work in staged increments:
Phase 0 — Discovery and fit: identify target school type, workflows, roles, paper forms, internet/device realities, current software, import data, fees, and Ministry reporting. Produce assumptions, risks, clickable workflow map, and prioritized requirements. Ask targeted questions only when answers change the architecture or policy.
Phase 1 — Foundation: tenant/school setup, campuses, roles, authentication, audit trail, core design system, accessibility baseline, import template, and demo data.
Phase 2 — School MVP: admissions, student records, academic setup, attendance, gradebook/report cards, fees/payments/balances, parent portal/announcements, staff directory, and essential reports.
Phase 3 — EMIS readiness: Ministry school code and field mapping, approved-template exports, validation, import preview, reconciliation logs, and documentation. Do not build an unapproved live connector.
Phase 4 — Pilot and hardening: pilot at one private school, train administrators/teachers, verify data migration, collect issues, improve workflows, test backups/offline scenarios/security, and document support procedures.
Phase 5 — Expansion: add colleges/universities, multiple campuses, payment-provider adapters, LMS/learning tools, payroll, transport, boarding, or other vertical modules based on paid customer demand.
At each phase, keep the app runnable. Provide setup instructions, environment-variable examples without real secrets, migration scripts, seeded demo data, test credentials for demo only, and a clear deployment guide.
9. Quality and acceptance criteria
Do not call the product ready because screens render. Verify complete workflows and data integrity.
A school administrator can configure an academic year, levels, class/sections, subjects, fees, and staff roles without developer assistance.
A registrar can import learners with validation, resolve duplicates, enroll learners, and produce a class roster.
A teacher can take attendance and enter marks only for assigned classes; corrections and approvals are audited.
A guardian can access only their linked learner(s), see released information, and cannot view unrelated learners or balances.
Finance staff can issue a charge, record a payment, issue a receipt, show the correct balance in the same currency, and reverse a mistaken payment through a logged approval flow.
An approved report card reflects the configured grading rules and cannot be changed without authorized reopening.
School and campus boundaries are tested for every major data type; cross-tenant access attempts fail.
EMIS export validates rows against a versioned template, identifies missing or invalid values, and never reports that data was sent to the Ministry when it was only downloaded.
Key screens work on common mobile widths; critical flows remain usable under simulated slow or interrupted connectivity.
Backups can be restored in a test environment; errors and logs do not expose private learner or payment data.
Automated tests cover critical business rules, permissions, balances, grade calculation, attendance uniqueness, imports/exports, and tenant isolation.
The school can export its own records in a practical, documented format when ending the service.
10. Required outputs from the engineering agent
Before substantial implementation, provide:
A concise product brief and target customer assumptions.
A functional requirements checklist and user-role/permission matrix.
A system architecture and data model, including tenant isolation and audit history.
A page/screen map and the end-to-end workflows for admissions, attendance, grades, fees, and parent access.
An EMIS compatibility plan distinguishing verified current behavior, configurable export/import, and future integration assumptions.
A prioritized MVP backlog with explicit “later” features.
A pilot plan including onboarding, paper-to-digital migration, training, support, and success measures.
Then implement the approved stages in the existing project, showing working screens and workflows, tests, setup instructions, and deployment notes. State any limitation honestly. Never fabricate Ministry approval, API availability, interoperability certification, payment-provider connectivity, legal compliance, customer adoption, or production readiness.
11. Product success measures
Instrument privacy-preserving metrics so a pilot can assess:
Time to enroll a learner and retrieve their record.
Attendance completion time and correction rate.
Grade-entry completion and report-card preparation time.
Fee reconciliation accuracy and outstanding-balance visibility.
Parent/guardian account activation and message delivery.
Import/export error rate and time required to prepare a Ministry report.
Weekly active school staff, support requests, and task completion rates.
Do not optimize for logins alone. The product succeeds when schools complete important work more accurately, promptly, and transparently than their current process.
12. Grounding and assumptions
Use current official Ministry documentation where available. As of October 2026, public MoE materials include a national EMIS statistics/reporting platform, an online school permit system that asks for a school EMIS code and school location/contact details, and ongoing work on EMIS policy and data standards. Regional EMIS discussions have described a national learner and teacher identifier system as a direction for integration and traceability. These public facts do not establish that a public API, approved third-party write access, or a final national identifier specification is available.
Treat each school’s supplied forms, Ministry-approved templates, and written instructions as authoritative for its implementation. Maintain a dated record of the source documents used for each EMIS mapping.
